#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229664 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229144 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "37e58bb6-6d90-5171-bba2-6ffa5a0c91d9" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Homogeneous Initial Value Problem Using a Given Matrix Exponential: Shifted Initial Conditions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "c00aedbd-3d32-591a-9238-e662e5a71296" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228182} {:answer-field/id #uuid "7397605e-8fc4-5376-8bac-4c5dbc127439" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228183}] :question/id #uuid "77f295d1-1cbb-5b17-a7ef-414238a16c0a" :question/math-academy-id "q-334547" :question/problem "${x^{′}(t) = 2x(t) + y(t); y^{′}(t) = 2y(t),\\begin{bmatrix}x(1) \\\\ y(1)\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: $e^{At} = \\begin{bmatrix}e^{2t} & te^{2t} \\\\ 0 & e^{2t}\\end{bmatrix}$, where $A = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$.*\n\n| $\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix} =$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372703 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229143 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3d611c75-818f-508f-a7a4-49edb32baa41" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Homogeneous Initial Value Problem Using a Given Matrix Exponential"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "911a0b91-8cfc-5c90-8612-d186b86260d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(4t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4t)\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372705} {:answer/id #uuid "ca7b866a-6af6-5c53-8767-9d73d4a5a6dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(4t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4t)\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372706} {:answer/id #uuid "f59f7b97-72d7-544d-92fb-affc25bc255f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(4t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4t)\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372707} {:answer/id #uuid "fba88690-6a3e-517e-b451-20f63869feb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4t)\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372708} {:answer/id #uuid "a1e0b67b-92e9-576c-93cc-05727469679d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(4t) \\\\ -2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4t)\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372709}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ca7b866a-6af6-5c53-8767-9d73d4a5a6dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(4t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4t)\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372706} :answer-field/id #uuid "1879bd9d-054e-59da-ab7b-0aabcf601f7a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372704}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "981edc7d-4bd0-566a-a596-3fb1014b4ef7" :question/math-academy-id "q-335264" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=4y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=4x(t),\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,\\begin{bmatrix}x(0) \\\\ y(0)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(4t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4t)\\end{bmatrix},$ where $A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.$*" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,x(0),\n$$\n\nwhere ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$ is the standard fundamental matrix of the system, and $x(0)$ is the initial condition vector.\n\nNow, using the given information, the standard fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(4t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4t)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{At}\\,x(0) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(4t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4t)\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(4t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4t)\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311564 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229144 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "37e58bb6-6d90-5171-bba2-6ffa5a0c91d9" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Homogeneous Initial Value Problem Using a Given Matrix Exponential: Shifted Initial Conditions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01f8dc2a-d1c1-50ad-9080-e4aea1133091" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{3(t-2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308761}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "01f8dc2a-d1c1-50ad-9080-e4aea1133091" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{3(t-2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308761} :answer-field/id #uuid "93eace97-bb5c-5bd2-b8df-7eca81320545" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311212} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07bdd633-a0bf-5ddf-ac96-58b9d4b0f277" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(t-2){e}^{3(t-2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308806}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "07bdd633-a0bf-5ddf-ac96-58b9d4b0f277" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(t-2){e}^{3(t-2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308806} :answer-field/id #uuid "e6cac817-7eaa-5d9c-919e-8afb3c166bfa" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311466}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "80bead61-fb6c-5bf6-88d2-511e52d01763" :question/math-academy-id "q-335273" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=3x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=3y(t),\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,\\begin{bmatrix}x(2) \\\\ y(2)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{3t} & t{e}^{3t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{3t}\\end{bmatrix},$ where $A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.$*\n\n| $\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the general solution of the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)=Φ(t)\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix of the system, and $c$ is a constant vector.\n\nSo, we can write our general solution as\n\n$$\nx(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)c={e}^{At}\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$ is the standard fundamental matrix of the system. Substituting $t={t}_{0},$ we have\n\n$$\nx({t}_{0})={e}^{A{t}_{0}}\\,c,\n$$\n\nand since ${e}^{At}$ is invertible for all $t,$ we have\n\n$$\nc={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}\\,x({t}_{0}).\n$$\n\nPlugging the above back into our expression for $x(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{At}\\,{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}\\,x({t}_{0}) \\\\  & ={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{A(t-2)}\\,x(2) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{3(t-2)} & (t-2){e}^{3(t-2)} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{3(t-2)}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\,(t-2){e}^{3(t-2)}\\, \\\\ \\,{e}^{3(t-2)}\\,\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372710 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229145 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1ff837c0-0ebb-5a9e-acb4-894261e0318c" :knowledge-point/title "Proving the Matrix Exponential Formula for IVP Solution: Homogeneous Systems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e8d581ab-eac2-55b5-888d-deaf7a002aed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "using properties of $det({Φ}_{0}(0))$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372712} {:answer/id #uuid "813ef154-d604-561e-b48e-0b35325f3927" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "applying the chain rule" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372713} {:answer/id #uuid "fdaf11ea-4164-580f-829e-d2abaeceb857" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "differentiating $x(0)={Φ}_{0}(0)c$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372714} {:answer/id #uuid "80ab58cb-932a-566d-b586-c196589d930b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "replacing ${Φ}_{0}(0)$ with ${I}_{n}$ in $x(0)={Φ}_{0}(0)c$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372715}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "80ab58cb-932a-566d-b586-c196589d930b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "replacing ${Φ}_{0}(0)$ with ${I}_{n}$ in $x(0)={Φ}_{0}(0)c$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372715} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4499e16-aabb-5cb2-a67c-ad7557ae05f6" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372711} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "853ace54-49de-53c0-82ed-cfb835e09e51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "evaluating both sides at $t=0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372717} {:answer/id #uuid "906f3949-ca05-5f13-8b47-f3dce789277e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "applying the chain rule" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372718} {:answer/id #uuid "9c8541f4-adab-5b02-9915-46d4efed7ca7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "using diagonalization of ${Φ}_{0}(t)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372719} {:answer/id #uuid "167e465a-c594-58b2-a782-69d90ccc3a9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "differentiating the equation" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372720}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "853ace54-49de-53c0-82ed-cfb835e09e51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "evaluating both sides at $t=0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372717} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0cfc463-a00c-5a02-b25e-b6454e6a70ed" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372716} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "484d762f-5acf-5df4-8df4-94cc90a1d4fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "properties of a fundamental matrix" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372722} {:answer/id #uuid "b3e78e24-6079-5e65-a2d8-6a25635330b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "definition of a solution curve" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372723} {:answer/id #uuid "8ddac18e-8996-5267-a9f1-7a3b6bca18c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "properties of the determinant" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372724} {:answer/id #uuid "12142844-889a-5ee2-b9bb-a31bbc51020a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "definition of matrix multiplication" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372725}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "484d762f-5acf-5df4-8df4-94cc90a1d4fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "properties of a fundamental matrix" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372722} :answer-field/id #uuid "3dea9463-8880-5a50-ac39-82e4de0d8f50" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372721}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "da2b3537-4a89-5afc-8ad7-721d1c3c30a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-336480" :question/problem "Consider the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix. You're given that the standard fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ is the unique solution to the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}^{′}(t)=A{Φ}_{0}(t),\\,{Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n}.\n$$\n\nA derivation of the formula for the solution $x(t)$ passing through $x(0)$ is presented below. \n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ Let ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ $x(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,c$ for some constant vector $c$\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ $x(0)={Φ}_{0}(0)\\,c$\n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ ${Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n}$\n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ $x(0)=c$\n\n$\\text{L6}:\\,$ $x(t)={e}^{At}\\,x(0)$\n\nSelect the correct options to justify each step of the reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L2}$ follows from $\\text{L1}$ by the \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L3}$ follows from $\\text{L2}$ by \n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L5}$ follows from lines $\\text{L3}$ and $\\text{L4}$ by \n{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first consider lines $\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L2}.$ Since ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$ is a fundamental matrix for the system, any solution of the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,c.\n$$\n\nThus, $\\text{L2}$ follows from $\\text{L1}$ by the $\\text{properties of a fundamental matrix}.$\n\n- Next, we consider lines $\\text{L2}$ and $\\text{L3}.$ From $\\text{L2}$ we have\n\n$$\nx(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,c.\n$$\n\nEvaluating this identity at $t=0$ gives\n\n$$\nx(0)={Φ}_{0}(0)c,\n$$\n\nwhich is precisely line $\\text{L3}.$ Thus, $\\text{L3}$ follows from $\\text{L2}$ by $\\text{evaluating both sides at }t=0.$\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\text{L3}$, $\\text{L4},$ and $\\text{L5}.$ From $\\text{L3}$ we know\n\n$$\nx(0)={Φ}_{0}(0)c,\n$$\n\nand from $\\text{L4}$ we know ${Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n}.$ Replacing ${Φ}_{0}(0)$ with ${I}_{n}$ in the equation $x(0)={Φ}_{0}(0)c$ gives\n\n$$\nx(0)={I}_{n}c=c,\n$$\n\nwhich is line $\\text{L5}.$ Therefore, $\\text{L5}$ follows from lines $\\text{L3}$ and $\\text{L4}$ by $\\text{replacing }{Φ}_{0}(0)\\text{ with }{I}_{n}\\text{ in }x(0)={Φ}_{0}(0)c.$"}]}
